GAD.reg("lesson","aud.nature.assurance",{"id":"aud.nature.assurance","title":"Assurance engagements and the engagement and quality control standards","md":"## In plain words\n\n### Three envelopes from Dev\n\nDev Malhotra, Tapri's CFO, walks into Iyer & Associates with three envelopes.\n\n> **Dev:** Lakshmi, the annual audit as usual. A quick look at the June quarter before we publish it. And a stamp on our three-year café forecast for an investor. I have already discounted every rupee in it.\n> **Lakshmi:** *(sighing)* The present value of that stamp is lower than you think, Dev. And these are three different engagements, not three audits.\n> **Bunty:** Ma'am, a CA signs at the bottom of all three. What is the difference?\n> **Lakshmi:** How much confidence the signature carries, Bunty, and how much work stands behind it.\n\nEach request asks a CA to look at information someone else prepared and tell other people how far they can trust it. That is an **assurance engagement**. Three parties are always involved: the CA (the **practitioner**), the people who prepared the information (the **responsible party**, here Tapri's management) and the people who will rely on the report (the **intended users**: shareholders, the investor, a bank).\n\nThe difference is one of degree. The **audit** of the annual financial statements performs extensive procedures and gives **reasonable assurance**, a high level. A **review** of the quarter performs fewer procedures and gives **limited assurance**, lower than an audit. The forecast is about the future, which no one can verify yet: the CA can only check whether its assumptions are not unreasonable and whether it is properly prepared and presented, and say that nothing suggests otherwise, a moderate level of assurance.\n\nICAI has a family of Standards for each kind of work: **Standards on Auditing** for audits, **Standards on Review Engagements** for reviews, **Standards on Assurance Engagements** for assurance on anything other than past financial information, and **Standards on Related Services** for jobs that give no assurance, such as compiling accounts for a client. Over all of them sits **SQC 1**, which makes the firm itself keep a system of quality control.\n\n::: analogy Buying a flat\nA structural engineer's full inspection before you buy is the audit: thorough, and you can rely on it. A friend who is an engineer walking through for an hour is the review: useful, but less comfort. The builder's brochure promising possession in 2028 is prospective information: an expert can tell you whether its assumptions look reasonable, never that the keys will arrive on time. And the clerk who fills in your loan form is a related service: helpful, but nobody is vouching for anything.\n:::\n\n::: key\nAn assurance engagement is a practitioner's conclusion that raises the confidence of intended users, other than the responsible party, in a subject matter measured against criteria. Audit gives reasonable (high) assurance, review limited (lower) assurance, prospective information only moderate assurance; related services give none. SAs, SREs, SAEs and SRSs are the Engagement Standards; SQC 1 applies to all of them.\n:::\n\n## How it works\n\n### 1. What an assurance engagement is\n\n::: definition Assurance engagement\n\"Assurance engagement\" means an engagement in which a practitioner expresses a conclusion designed to enhance the degree of confidence of the intended users other than the responsible party about the outcome of the evaluation or measurement of a subject matter against criteria (SM 1.22).\n:::\n\nIt means that the practitioner gives an opinion about specific information due to which users of information are able to make confident decisions, knowing well that the chance of the information being incorrect is diminished (SM 1.22). An audit is one type of assurance engagement, in which the auditor gives an opinion on whether the financial statements give a true and fair view; but assurance engagements are not restricted to audit alone (SM 1.22).\n\n### 2. The five elements\n\nThe following elements comprise an assurance engagement, in this order (SM 1.23–1.24):\n\n1. **A three party relationship involving a practitioner, a responsible party and intended users.** A **practitioner** is the person who provides the assurance. The term practitioner is broader than auditor: audit is related to historical information, whereas a practitioner may provide assurance not necessarily related to historical financial information. A **responsible party** is the party responsible for preparation of the subject matter. **Intended users** are the persons for whom the assurance report is prepared; they may use it in making decisions.\n2. **An appropriate subject matter**: the information to be examined by the practitioner, for example the financial information contained in financial statements in an audit of financial statements.\n3. **Suitable criteria**: the benchmarks used to evaluate the subject matter, like standards, guidance, laws, rules and regulations.\n4. **Sufficient appropriate evidence.** The practitioner performs an assurance engagement to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence; it is on the basis of evidence that conclusions are arrived at and an opinion is formed. \"Sufficient\" relates to the **quantity** of evidence, \"appropriate\" to its **quality**: evidence that gives the auditor more comfort is qualitative and so appropriate. Evidence should be both.\n5. **A written assurance report in appropriate form**, containing a conclusion that conveys the assurance about the subject matter. The written assurance report is the outcome of an assurance engagement.\n\n::: diagram The three party relationship in Tapri's audit\n{\"type\": \"graph\", \"w\": 420, \"h\": 310, \"nodes\": [{\"id\": \"rp\", \"label\": \"Responsible\\nparty\", \"note\": \"Tapri's management\", \"x\": 75, \"y\": 70, \"r\": 40, \"fill\": \"c\"}, {\"id\": \"sm\", \"label\": \"Subject\\nmatter\", \"x\": 345, \"y\": 70, \"r\": 40, \"fill\": \"mute\"}, {\"id\": \"pr\", \"label\": \"Practitioner\", \"note\": \"Iyer & Associates\", \"x\": 345, \"y\": 235, \"r\": 40, \"fill\": \"a\"}, {\"id\": \"iu\", \"label\": \"Intended\\nusers\", \"note\": \"shareholders, lenders\", \"x\": 75, \"y\": 235, \"r\": 40, \"fill\": \"g\"}], \"edges\": [{\"from\": \"rp\", \"to\": \"sm\", \"label\": \"prepares\"}, {\"from\": \"pr\", \"to\": \"sm\", \"label\": \"evidence,\\ncriteria\"}, {\"from\": \"pr\", \"to\": \"iu\", \"label\": \"written report\"}], \"caption\": \"Three parties and the subject matter, which in Tapri's audit is the financial statements. The practitioner evaluates the subject matter against suitable criteria (for a company, the applicable financial reporting framework), obtains sufficient appropriate evidence and reports in writing to the intended users, who are users other than the responsible party.\"}\n:::\n\n### 3. Audit and review: the same information, different degrees\n\nAudit is a **reasonable assurance engagement**: it provides reasonable assurance. **Review** is a **limited assurance engagement**: it provides a lower level of assurance than audit. Review involves fewer procedures and gathers sufficient appropriate evidence on the basis of which limited conclusions can be drawn. Both \"audit\" and \"review\" are related to financial statements prepared on the basis of historical financial information (SM 1.24).\n\nAssurance engagements provide assurance to users; the difference is of degree. Reasonable assurance, as in an audit, is a high level of assurance; limited assurance, as in a review, is lower than audit, only a moderate level (SM 1.24):\n\n::: compare Reasonable assurance engagement and limited assurance engagement (SM 1.25)\n| Reasonable assurance engagement | Limited assurance engagement |\n|---|---|\n| Provides a high level of assurance | Provides a lower level of assurance than a reasonable assurance engagement |\n| Performs elaborate and extensive procedures to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence | Performs fewer procedures as compared to a reasonable assurance engagement |\n| Draws reasonable conclusions on the basis of sufficient appropriate evidence | Involves obtaining sufficient appropriate evidence to draw limited conclusions |\n| Example: an audit engagement | Example: a review engagement |\n:::\n\n*Reproduce this table from memory, row by row: it is ICAI's own answer to \"distinguish\".* Note the third row: a review still obtains ==sufficient appropriate evidence==; what is limited is the conclusion.\n\n### 4. Assurance on matters other than historical financial information\n\nBesides reasonable and limited assurance engagements, there is another kind of assurance, related to matters other than historical financial information: prospective financial information, or assurance on internal controls in an entity (SM 1.25).\n\n::: definition Prospective financial information\n\"Prospective financial information\" means financial information based on assumptions about events that may occur in the future and possible actions by an entity. It can be in the form of a forecast or projection or combination of both (SM 1.25).\n:::\n\n::: compare Historical and prospective financial information (SM 1.11, 1.25)\n| Point | Historical financial information | Prospective financial information |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it is | Expressed in financial terms, about economic events, conditions or circumstances of past periods | Based on assumptions about the occurrence of future events and possible actions by an entity |\n| Rooted in | Past events that have already occurred | Future events |\n| Example | Purchases and sales in the financial statements | A forecast or projection |\n| Engagements | Audit (SAs) and review (SREs) | Examination under the SAEs |\n:::\n\n**What the practitioner does with a forecast** (SM 1.26). In assurance reports involving prospective financial information, the practitioner obtains sufficient appropriate evidence that:\n\n- management's assumptions on which the prospective financial information is based are **not unreasonable**;\n- the prospective financial information is **properly prepared** on the basis of the assumptions;\n- it is **properly presented**; and\n- all material assumptions are **adequately disclosed**.\n\nEvidence may be available to support the assumptions, but such evidence is itself generally future-oriented. The auditor is therefore **not in a position to express an opinion as to whether the results shown will be achieved**. So the practitioner provides a report assuring that **nothing has come to the practitioner's attention to suggest that these assumptions do not provide a reasonable basis for the projection**. Hence such an engagement provides only a **\"moderate\" level of assurance** (SM 1.26).\n\n### 5. The map of assurance engagements\n\n| Example of assurance engagement | Type of assurance engagement (SM 1.26) |\n|---|---|\n| Audit of financial statements | Reasonable assurance engagement |\n| Review of financial statements | Limited assurance engagement |\n| Examination of prospective financial information | Assurance regarding reasonability of assumptions forming the basis of projections and related matters |\n| Report on controls operating at an organisation | Assurance regarding design and operation of controls |\n\n::: diagram Assurance engagements (SM 1.27)\n{\"type\": \"tree\", \"gapX\": 26, \"gapY\": 80, \"root\": {\"label\": \"Assurance\\nengagements\", \"children\": [{\"label\": \"Reasonable\\nassurance\", \"children\": [{\"label\": \"Audit\"}]}, {\"label\": \"Limited\\nassurance\", \"children\": [{\"label\": \"Review\"}]}, {\"label\": \"Other than\\nhistorical\\nfinancial info\", \"children\": [{\"label\": \"Prospective\\ninformation;\\ncontrols\"}]}]}, \"caption\": \"The Study Material's three kinds. The first two deal with historical financial information; the third with forecasts and projections, or with the operation of controls.\"}\n:::\n\n### 6. The engagement and quality control standards\n\nThe following Standards issued under the authority of the ICAI Council are collectively known as **Engagement Standards** (SM 1.28):\n\n1. **Standards on Auditing (SAs)**, which apply in audit of historical financial information.\n2. **Standards on Review Engagements (SREs)**, which apply in review of historical financial information.\n3. **Standards on Assurance Engagements (SAEs)**, which apply in assurance engagements other than audits and review of historical financial information.\n4. **Standards on Related Services (SRSs)**, which apply in agreed upon procedures to information, compilation engagements and other related service engagements.\n\nThe purpose of issue of these standards is ==to establish high quality standards and guidance in the areas of financial statement audits and in other types of assurance services== (SM 1.28). Engagement Standards deal with the responsibilities of the auditor or practitioner (SM 1.31).\n\n**Standards on Auditing** apply in the context of an audit of financial statements by an independent auditor, in audit of historical information. They establish high quality benchmarks and cover all significant aspects of an audit, from the overall objectives of the independent auditor, audit documentation, planning, identifying and assessing risks of material misstatement, audit sampling and audit evidence to forming an opinion and reporting. Examples: SA 200 (Overall Objectives of the Independent Auditor and the Conduct of an Audit in accordance with Standards on Auditing), SA 230 (Audit Documentation), SA 315 (Identifying and Assessing the Risks of Material Misstatement through Understanding the Entity and its Environment), SA 500 (Audit Evidence), Revised SA 700 (Forming an Opinion and Reporting on Financial Statements) (SM 1.29).\n\n**Standards on Review Engagements** apply in the context of review of financial statements. Review provides lower assurance than audit because it involves fewer procedures; since it also provides assurance, it too involves obtaining sufficient appropriate evidence, for example when an auditor performs a review of interim financial information of an entity. Examples: SRE 2400 (Revised) Engagements to Review Historical Financial Statements; SRE 2410 Review of Interim Financial Information Performed by the Independent Auditor of the Entity. Both SAs and SREs apply to engagements involving historical financial information (SM 1.29–1.30).\n\n**Standards on Assurance Engagements** apply in assurance engagements dealing with subject matters other than historical financial information: they do not include audit or review of historical financial information. Example: an examination of prospective financial information; or the engagement may relate to non-financial matters like the design and operation of internal control in an entity. Examples: SAE 3400 The Examination of Prospective Financial Information; SAE 3420 Assurance Engagements to Report on the Compilation of Pro Forma Financial Information Included in a Prospectus (SM 1.30).\n\n**Standards on Related Services** apply in (SM 1.30–1.31):\n\n- **Agreed-upon procedures** regarding financial information. Such an engagement may require the auditor to perform certain procedures on individual items of financial data (accounts payable, accounts receivable, purchases from related parties, sales and profits of a segment of an entity), on a financial statement (say, a balance sheet) or even on a complete set of financial statements.\n- **Compilation engagements**, in which the practitioner is called upon to assist management with the preparation and presentation of historical financial information **without obtaining assurance** on that information. The practitioner issues a report clearly stating that it is not an assurance engagement and no opinion is being expressed.\n\nExamples: SRS 4400 Engagements to perform agreed-upon procedures regarding financial information; SRS 4410 (Revised) Compilation engagements.\n\n**Standards on Quality Control (SQCs)** have been issued to establish standards and provide guidance regarding a firm's responsibilities for its system of quality control for the conduct of audit and review of historical financial information and for other assurance and related service engagements. **SQC 1** requires auditors and practitioners to establish a system of quality control so that the firm and its personnel comply with professional standards and regulatory and legal requirements, and that reports issued are appropriate. ==SQCs are to be applied for all services covered by Engagement Standards== (SM 1.31). Its full title says as much: SQC 1 \"Quality Control for Firms that Perform Audits and Reviews of Historical Financial Information, and Other Assurance and Related Services Engagements\" (SM 11.25; [[aud.ethics.qc|SQC 1 and SA 220]]).\n\n::: diagram The engagement and quality control standards\n{\"type\": \"nest\", \"w\": 420, \"root\": {\"title\": \"SQC 1\", \"note\": \"the firm's quality control\", \"color\": \"mute\", \"mono\": false, \"children\": [{\"title\": \"Engagement Standards\", \"note\": \"the practitioner's duties\", \"color\": \"a\", \"mono\": false, \"children\": [{\"title\": \"SAs\", \"note\": \"audit\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"a\", \"lines\": [\"historical financial information\", \"SA 200, 230, 315, 500, 700\"]}, {\"title\": \"SREs\", \"note\": \"review\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"b\", \"lines\": [\"historical financial information\", \"SRE 2400 (Revised), SRE 2410\"]}, {\"title\": \"SAEs\", \"note\": \"other assurance\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"c\", \"lines\": [\"prospective information, controls\", \"SAE 3400, SAE 3420\"]}, {\"title\": \"SRSs\", \"note\": \"related services\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"d\", \"lines\": [\"agreed-upon procedures, compilation\", \"SRS 4400, SRS 4410 (Revised)\"]}]}]}, \"caption\": \"The outer box contains everything inside it: SQC 1 applies to the firm for every engagement the four families of Engagement Standards cover (SM 1.28–1.31).\"}\n:::\n\n### 7. Which Standard applies?\n\n::: diagram Choosing the family of Standards\n{\"type\": \"flow\", \"w\": 380, \"nodes\": [{\"id\": \"s\", \"kind\": \"start\", \"label\": \"An engagement\\noffered to a CA\"}, {\"id\": \"d1\", \"kind\": \"decision\", \"label\": \"An assurance\\nengagement?\"}, {\"id\": \"srs\", \"kind\": \"end\", \"label\": \"SRSs: related\\nservices\"}, {\"id\": \"d2\", \"kind\": \"decision\", \"label\": \"Audit or\\nreview of\\nHFI?\"}, {\"id\": \"sae\", \"kind\": \"end\", \"label\": \"SAEs: other\\nassurance\"}, {\"id\": \"d3\", \"kind\": \"decision\", \"label\": \"Which\\none?\"}, {\"id\": \"sa\", \"kind\": \"end\", \"label\": \"SAs: reasonable\\nassurance\"}, {\"id\": \"sre\", \"kind\": \"end\", \"label\": \"SREs: limited\\nassurance\"}], \"edges\": [[\"s\", \"d1\"], [\"d1\", \"srs\", \"no\"], [\"d1\", \"d2\", \"yes\"], [\"d2\", \"sae\", \"no\"], [\"d2\", \"d3\", \"yes\"], [\"d3\", \"sa\", \"audit\"], [\"d3\", \"sre\", \"review\"]], \"caption\": \"HFI: historical financial information. SRSs cover agreed-upon procedures and compilation engagements; SAEs cover, for example, prospective financial information and controls. Whichever end the engagement reaches, SQC 1 also applies to the firm.\"}\n:::\n\n### 8. Why Standards are needed\n\nThe Study Material gives five reasons (SM 1.31–1.32):\n\n1. Standards ensure carrying out of audit against established benchmarks at par with global practices.\n2. Standards improve quality of financial reporting, thereby helping users to make diligent decisions.\n3. Standards promote uniformity, as audit of financial statements is carried out following these Standards.\n4. Standards equip professional accountants with professional knowledge and skill.\n5. Standards ensure audit quality.\n\n### 9. Following the Standards, and departing from a procedure\n\nIt is the duty of professional accountants to see that Standards are followed in engagements undertaken by them. Ordinarily they are to be followed; but a situation may arise in which a specific procedure required by a Standard would be ineffective in a particular engagement. Then (SM 1.32):\n\n::: steps\n1. **Document** how the alternative procedures performed achieve the purpose of the required procedure.\n2. **Document the reason for the departure**, unless it is clear.\n3. **The report should draw attention** to such departures.\n4. Remember that **a mere disclosure in the report does not absolve** a professional accountant from complying with applicable Standards.\n:::\n\n## Worked examples\n\n:::: example Four assignments, four families\nIyer & Associates receive the following requests in September 2026. For each, identify the nature of the engagement, the Engagement Standards issued by ICAI that apply (with the Standard named in the Study Material, where there is one), and the level of assurance, if any. Also state whether SQC 1 applies.\n\n(a) Shubh Labh Foods LLP, a Tapri franchisee, wants its annual financial statements reviewed for a landlord who is also an investor. The firm is not its auditor and will perform fewer procedures than an audit.\n\n(b) Mehra Masala Traders, a partnership firm run by Kesar's cousin Pinky, has lost its accountant. It asks the firm to prepare its financial statements for the year from the trial balance and records, without giving any assurance on them.\n\n(c) Kulhad Crafts Ltd asks the firm to recompute, for twelve months, the commission it paid each of its 40 sales agents at 3% of the agent's sales, and to report the differences found.\n\n(d) TapriNow Pvt Ltd, Tapri's delivery-app subsidiary, is converting into a public company for a public issue; its prospectus includes pro forma financial information showing the effect of acquiring a rival delivery app; the merchant banker wants a CA's report on its compilation.\n\n::: answer Model answer\n**Relevant provision.** Engagement Standards issued under the authority of the ICAI Council comprise Standards on Auditing (audit of historical financial information), Standards on Review Engagements (review of historical financial information), Standards on Assurance Engagements (assurance engagements other than audit and review of historical financial information) and Standards on Related Services (agreed-upon procedures, compilation and other related services). Standards on Quality Control are to be applied for all services covered by Engagement Standards.\n\n| Request | Nature | Standards | Assurance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) Review of the LLP's annual financial statements | Review of historical financial information | SREs: SRE 2400 (Revised) | Limited |\n| (b) Preparing Mehra Masala Traders' financial statements | Compilation engagement | SRSs: SRS 4410 (Revised) | None: a related service |\n| (c) Recomputing agents' commission | Agreed-upon procedures regarding financial information | SRSs: SRS 4400 | None: a related service |\n| (d) Pro forma information in a prospectus | Assurance engagement other than audit or review of historical financial information | SAEs: SAE 3420 | Assurance on the compilation |\n\n**Analysis.**\n\n(a) A review performs fewer procedures than an audit but still obtains sufficient appropriate evidence, on which limited conclusions are drawn: a limited assurance engagement, lower than the reasonable assurance of an audit.\n\n(b) Assisting management with the preparation and presentation of historical financial information without obtaining assurance is a compilation engagement. Iyer & Associates' report must state clearly that it is not an assurance engagement and that no opinion is expressed.\n\n(c) Performing specified procedures on an individual item of financial data (the agents' commission) is an engagement to perform agreed-upon procedures regarding financial information: a related service, not an assurance engagement.\n\n(d) The Study Material names SAE 3420, Assurance Engagements to Report on the Compilation of Pro Forma Financial Information Included in a Prospectus, as a Standard on Assurance Engagements.\n\n**Conclusion.** SQC 1 applies to all four: it is applied for all services covered by Engagement Standards, including compilations and agreed-upon procedures, and requires the firm to have a system of quality control so that the firm and its personnel comply with professional standards and legal requirements and its reports are appropriate.\n:::\n::::\n\n::::: example The forecast that grows like a hockey stick\nKesar wants equity from Narmada Growth Partners to expand Tapri's highway cafés, the chain it runs at petrol pumps. Tapri prepares a three-year forecast and engages CA Farah Sheikh to report on it. The historical figures and the forecast are given below:\n\n| Year | Cafés | Revenue per café (₹ lakh) | Revenue (₹ crore) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2023-24 (actual) | 120 | 27.0 | 32.40 |\n| 2024-25 (actual) | 140 | 28.5 | 39.90 |\n| 2025-26 (actual) | 160 | 30.0 | 48.00 |\n| 2026-27 (forecast) | 240 | 40.0 | 96.00 |\n| 2027-28 (forecast) | 320 | 55.0 | 176.00 |\n| 2028-29 (forecast) | 400 | 75.0 | 300.00 |\n\nKesar asks CA Farah to \"certify that Tapri's highway-café revenue will reach ₹300 crore in 2028-29\".\n\nRequired:\n\n(i) Which Engagement Standards apply, and what level of assurance can the report give?\n\n(ii) Can CA Farah give the certificate Kesar wants? What must she obtain evidence about, and what will her report say?\n\n(iii) Comment, with workings, on the forecast's assumption about revenue per café.\n\n:::: answer Model answer\n**(i) Standards and assurance.** A forecast is prospective financial information: financial information based on assumptions about events that may occur in the future and possible actions by an entity. Its examination is an assurance engagement on a matter other than historical financial information, governed by the Standards on Assurance Engagements (SAE 3400, The Examination of Prospective Financial Information), with SQC 1 applying to the firm. Such an engagement provides only a moderate level of assurance.\n\n**(ii) The certificate.** Prospective financial information relates to future events. Evidence may be available to support the assumptions, but such evidence is itself generally future-oriented, so the practitioner is not in a position to express an opinion as to whether the results shown will be achieved. CA Farah cannot certify that revenue will reach ₹300 crore.\n\nShe will obtain sufficient appropriate evidence that management's assumptions are not unreasonable (which includes testing their consistency with the actual results), that the forecast is properly prepared on the basis of the assumptions, that it is properly presented, and that all material assumptions are adequately disclosed. Her report will state that nothing has come to her attention to suggest that the assumptions do not provide a reasonable basis for the forecast.\n\n**(iii) Revenue per café.** Working note:\n\n| Year | Revenue per café (₹ lakh) | Growth on previous year |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2024-25 (actual) | 28.5 | 5.6% |\n| 2025-26 (actual) | 30.0 | 5.3% |\n| 2026-27 (forecast) | 40.0 | 33.3% |\n| 2027-28 (forecast) | 55.0 | 37.5% |\n| 2028-29 (forecast) | 75.0 | 36.4% |\n\n- Revenue per café grew about 5.4% a year over the two actual years; the forecast assumes about 35.7% a year, 2.5 times in three years.\n- Total revenue rises 6.25 times, from ₹48 crore to ₹300 crore (about 84.2% a year), against about 20–23% a year in the actual years.\n- At the historical growth of about 5.4% a year, 400 cafés would earn about ₹35.14 lakh each in 2028-29, a revenue of about ₹140.5 crore. About ₹159.5 crore of the ₹300 crore therefore rests on the revenue-per-café assumption alone.\n\nThis assumption is inconsistent with the actual results. CA Farah must obtain sufficient appropriate evidence supporting it (for example, the basis for the jump in sales per café) and see that it is adequately disclosed. Unless it is supported, she could not report that nothing has come to her attention to suggest that the assumptions do not provide a reasonable basis for the forecast.\n::::\n:::::\n\n:::: example The refund-controls report\nTapriNow Pvt Ltd, Tapri's subsidiary, runs the TapriNow delivery app. Its payment partner, PayKaro Ltd, asks for an independent report on whether TapriNow's controls over customer refunds were suitably designed and operated during 2025-26, measured against the control requirements in the partner agreement. Iyer & Associates are engaged. TapriNow's internal audit team, which has already tested the controls, offers its working papers.\n\nBunty drafts the planning note: \"Three parties: Iyer & Associates, TapriNow's internal audit team and PayKaro. Subject matter: TapriNow's financial statements. Evidence: we can rely on the internal audit team's conclusion that the controls work.\"\n\nIdentify the elements of this assurance engagement, correcting Bunty's note where needed, and state which Engagement Standards apply.\n\n::: answer Model answer\n**Relevant provision.** An assurance engagement is one in which a practitioner expresses a conclusion designed to enhance the degree of confidence of the intended users other than the responsible party about the outcome of the evaluation or measurement of a subject matter against criteria. Its elements are a three party relationship, an appropriate subject matter, suitable criteria, sufficient appropriate evidence and a written assurance report in appropriate form.\n\n**Elements in the case.**\n\n1. **Three party relationship.** Practitioner: Iyer & Associates, who provide the assurance. Responsible party: the management of TapriNow, responsible for the subject matter, the refund controls it designed and operates. Intended users: PayKaro Ltd, for whom the report is prepared. The internal audit team is part of TapriNow, the responsible party's own organisation, not a separate party: Bunty's list is incorrect.\n2. **Subject matter.** The design and operation of the refund controls during 2025-26, not the financial statements: Bunty's note is incorrect.\n3. **Suitable criteria.** The control requirements set out in the partner agreement, the benchmark against which the controls are evaluated.\n4. **Sufficient appropriate evidence.** Iyer & Associates must obtain sufficient (enough in quantity) and appropriate (of good quality) evidence on which to base their conclusion. The internal audit team's conclusion comes from within the responsible party; it does not replace the evidence the practitioner must obtain, so Bunty's last line is also incorrect.\n5. **Written assurance report.** A written report in appropriate form, containing a conclusion that conveys the assurance about the design and operation of the controls.\n\n**Standards.** Assurance regarding the design and operation of controls is an assurance engagement on a matter other than historical financial information: the Standards on Assurance Engagements apply, together with SQC 1 for the firm.\n:::\n::::\n\n## Under the hood\n\n### Evidence is not optional in a review or a forecast\n\n\"Sufficient appropriate evidence\" is an element of every assurance engagement, not just audits. ICAI's RTP for January 2026 (MCQ 2) marks as correct that it is to be obtained in audit engagements, review engagements and engagements involving examination of prospective financial information. The Study Material's answer to a client who wants a quarterly assurance report \"by skipping required detailed procedures\": the report would be in the nature of a review, and the auditors would still have to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence (SM 1.27, 1.41).\n\n### Assurance engagement risk\n\nRTP May 2024 (Q2) adds a sentence the chapter itself does not: audit, a reasonable assurance engagement, aims at reducing assurance engagement risk to an acceptably low level in the circumstances; a review, a limited assurance engagement, at reducing it to a level that is acceptable in the circumstances. Use it to enrich an \"SA versus SRE\" answer; the four-row table is still the core.\n\n### When the client wants to switch\n\nA client who asks, mid-audit, to turn the audit into a review (an engagement conveying a lower level of assurance) is dealt with by SA 210: the auditor needs reasonable justification before agreeing, and a request to avoid a qualified opinion or disclaimer is not one (SM 11.22–11.23; [[aud.ethics.sa210|SA 210]]).\n\n### SQC 1 and SA 220\n\nBoth deal with quality control. SQC 1 deals with all engagements, including audits, reviews and other assurance and related service engagements, and applies to the entire firm; SA 220 applies to audit engagements only, to a particular audit engagement (SM 11.24; [[aud.ethics.qc|SQC 1 and SA 220]]).\n\n## Exam angle\n\n::: exam Where these topics appeared, May 2024 to September 2026\nIn the ICAI documents read for this lesson, assurance engagements and the engagement standards were examined in 15 of them:\n\n- **Prospective and historical financial information, 4–5 marks**: January 2025 Q2(a), 5 marks (\"explain the key differences … how does this distinction impact the level of assurance\"); MTP January 2025 Series I Q2(c), 4 marks (applicable Standard and level of assurance for a revenue projection); MTP September 2025 Series II Q5(b), 5 marks.\n- **Which Standard applies, and reasonable versus limited**: January 2026 Q1(b), 5 marks (a compilation and an examination of prospective information, then the difference between reasonable and limited assurance, 3 marks); MTP May 2025 Series I Q5(c), 4 marks; MTP May 2024 Series II Q1(d), 3 marks (features of a limited assurance engagement); RTP May 2024 Q2 (SAs versus SREs).\n- **Engagement Standards and their purpose**: May 2025 Q2(a), 5 marks (\"mention any three standards … collectively known as Engagement Standards\", 3 marks, and their purpose, 2 marks).\n- **Meaning, elements, three parties**: RTP September 2026 Q9 (meaning, identify the parties, discuss the elements); MTP September 2024 Series II Q2(a), 5 marks (three examples showing different assurance); RTP September 2024 Q9 (are assurance engagements restricted to audit?).\n- **Multiple choice, 2 marks**: the three party relationship (January 2026 MCQ 6, a CSR report; RTP January 2026 MCQ 4, where bankers and income tax authorities are the distractors); agreed-upon procedures, answered \"Standards on Related Services and SQC 1\" (MTP January 2025 Series I MCQ 15; MTP September 2024 Series II MCQ 14); projections, answered \"Standards on Assurance Engagements\" (MTP May 2025 Series II MCQ 14; September 2026 MCQ 10, answers not yet published); the statement that is NOT true: \"a limited assurance engagement involves obtaining only limited evidence\" (MTP September 2026 Series II MCQ 15).\n:::\n\n::: exam The traps\nThree wrong options that recur in ICAI's MCQs and in the Study Material's Case Study 2: a limited assurance engagement obtains \"only limited evidence\" (it obtains sufficient appropriate evidence and draws limited conclusions); the third party is the bankers, the income tax authorities or the internal audit staff (it is the intended users); SQC 1 does not apply because no audit report is issued, or the work follows the SRSs \"and SA 220\" (SQC 1 applies to every service covered by Engagement Standards; SA 220 applies to audits only).\n:::\n\n## Answer frame\n\n### \"Which Standards apply, and what assurance?\" (a case)\n\n1. **Provision**: the four families of Engagement Standards, with where each applies, and \"SQCs are to be applied for all services covered by Engagement Standards\".\n2. **Facts**: what is examined (historical or prospective; financial or controls) and whether a conclusion giving assurance is expressed.\n3. **Analysis**: the family; the Standard the Study Material names, if any (SRE 2400 (Revised), SRE 2410, SAE 3400, SAE 3420, SRS 4400, SRS 4410 (Revised)); the level of assurance.\n4. **Conclusion**: \"Hence, the Standards on … are relevant; SQC 1 applies\".\n\n### \"Distinguish reasonable and limited assurance engagements\"\n\nThe four rows of ICAI's table: level of assurance; procedures (elaborate and extensive, or fewer); conclusions (reasonable, or limited, both on sufficient appropriate evidence); example (audit, or review).\n\n### \"Distinguish historical and prospective financial information\" and its effect on assurance\n\nDefinitions (past events that have occurred; assumptions about future events and possible actions), then the four things the practitioner obtains evidence on, why no opinion on achievement is possible (future-oriented evidence), the \"nothing has come to the practitioner's attention\" report, and \"moderate\".\n\n### \"Discuss the elements of an assurance engagement\"\n\nThe five in ICAI's order, one line each, with the three parties defined.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n::: mistake Hall of Shame: \"limited assurance, so limited evidence\"\nBunty writes that a review needs less than sufficient appropriate evidence. **Fix:** a limited assurance engagement involves obtaining **sufficient appropriate evidence** to draw **limited conclusions**; it performs fewer procedures (SM 1.25). This was the wrong statement in MTP September 2026 Series II MCQ 15.\n:::\n\n::: mistake The bank as the third party\nNaming the practitioner, management and \"the bank\" or \"the income tax authorities\" as the three parties. **Fix:** the third party is the **intended users**, the persons for whom the report is prepared, other than the responsible party.\n:::\n\n::: mistake SQC 1 only for audits\nSaying SQC 1 does not apply to a compilation because no opinion is issued. **Fix:** SQCs are to be applied for all services covered by Engagement Standards, related services included (SM 1.31; Case Study 2 answer (c), SM 1.36).\n:::\n\n::: mistake Certifying the future\nWriting that the practitioner confirms a forecast \"will be achieved\" or checks only its arithmetic. **Fix:** he obtains evidence that the assumptions are not unreasonable, the forecast is properly prepared and presented and material assumptions are disclosed; he cannot give an opinion on achievement.\n:::\n\n::: mistake \"Assurance means historical financial information\"\n**Fix:** assurance engagements are not restricted to audit, nor to historical financial information: prospective information and controls are subject matters of assurance engagements too (SM 1.25, 1.38–1.40).\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n::: quiz Check yourself\nQ: Case scenario. Tapri borrows ₹50 crore under a green loan whose agreement lists the only kinds of spending allowed: solar panels, electric delivery vans and effluent treatment. Tapri's management prepares a statement of how the money was spent, and the lender asks Iyer & Associates for an assurance report on that statement. Bunty is labelling the elements of the engagement. Which pairing is correct?\n- [ ] Subject matter: the loan agreement's list of allowed spending; criteria: management's statement of spending\n- [x] Subject matter: management's statement of how the ₹50 crore was spent; criteria: the loan agreement's list of allowed spending\n- [ ] Subject matter: the vendor invoices and bank statements; criteria: the lender's credit policy\n- [ ] Subject matter: Iyer & Associates' report; criteria: SQC 1\n> The subject matter is the information to be examined; the criteria are the benchmarks used to evaluate it (standards, guidance, laws, rules, regulations: here the agreement's terms) (SM 1.23). Invoices and bank statements are evidence, the report is the fifth element, and SQC 1 is the firm's quality control.\n\nQ: Case scenario. Iyer & Associates, Tapri's statutory auditors, are asked to review Tapri's June-quarter financial results before they are published. Which Standard named in the Study Material fits, and with what result?\n- [ ] SRE 2400 (Revised); reasonable assurance, as in the annual audit\n- [ ] SA 700 (Revised); an audit opinion on the quarter\n- [x] SRE 2410; limited assurance, lower than an audit, on sufficient appropriate evidence\n- [ ] SRE 2410; limited assurance, so sufficient appropriate evidence is not needed\n> SRE 2410 is \"Review of Interim Financial Information Performed by the Independent Auditor of the Entity\" (SM 1.29). A review is a limited assurance engagement, but it still involves obtaining sufficient appropriate evidence; what is limited is the conclusion (SM 1.25, 1.29).\n\nQ: Bunty photocopies 400 delivery challans for a sample of 20 refunds and announces: \"Four hundred pages, Ma'am. My evidence is now appropriate.\" Which statement is correct?\n- [ ] He is right: more evidence is more appropriate evidence\n- [ ] \"Appropriate\" relates to the quantity of evidence and \"sufficient\" to its quality\n- [ ] Only audits need appropriate evidence; a review needs only sufficient evidence\n- [x] \"Sufficient\" relates to the quantity of evidence and \"appropriate\" to its quality; volume speaks only to sufficiency, and evidence must be both\n> SM 1.23: \"Sufficient\" relates to quantity, \"appropriate\" to quality (the evidence that gives more comfort is qualitative and so appropriate), and evidence should be both sufficient and appropriate, in every assurance engagement.\n\nQ: A practitioner examining a five-year projection obtains evidence on several matters. Which of these is NOT one of them?\n- [ ] Management's assumptions are not unreasonable\n- [x] The projected results will be achieved\n- [ ] The projection is properly prepared on the basis of the assumptions\n- [ ] All material assumptions are adequately disclosed\n> Evidence about the future is itself future-oriented, so no opinion on achievement is possible; the report says nothing has come to the practitioner's attention to suggest the assumptions do not provide a reasonable basis.\n\nQ: Which are collectively known as the Engagement Standards? Select all that apply.\n- [x] Standards on Auditing\n- [x] Standards on Review Engagements\n- [x] Standards on Related Services\n- [ ] Standards on Quality Control\n> The four Engagement Standards are SAs, SREs, SAEs and SRSs. SQCs are separate: they deal with the firm's system of quality control and apply to all services the Engagement Standards cover.\n\nQ: Case scenario. With the board meeting two days away, the audit team skips a procedure an SA requires for Tapri's audit, simply because time has run out, and the senior proposes a line in the audit report mentioning it. Which is correct?\n- [ ] Acceptable: drawing attention to the departure in the report is enough\n- [ ] Acceptable, provided the reason for the departure is documented\n- [x] Not acceptable: the Study Material allows a departure where a required procedure would be ineffective in the engagement, with alternative procedures documented as achieving its purpose; a mere disclosure in the report does not absolve the auditor from complying\n- [ ] Not acceptable, and the auditor must withdraw from the engagement\n> It is the duty of professional accountants to see that Standards are followed. A departure arises when a specific procedure would be ineffective; then the auditor documents how the alternative procedures achieve its purpose and the reason for departure (unless clear), and the report draws attention to it. Shortage of time is not that situation, and disclosure alone never absolves (SM 1.32).\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n- Assurance engagement: a practitioner expresses a conclusion designed to enhance the degree of confidence of intended users other than the responsible party about the evaluation or measurement of a subject matter against criteria.\n- Five elements: a three party relationship (practitioner, responsible party, intended users), an appropriate subject matter, suitable criteria, sufficient appropriate evidence and a written assurance report in appropriate form.\n- Audit: reasonable assurance (high), elaborate and extensive procedures. Review: limited assurance (lower), fewer procedures, still sufficient appropriate evidence, limited conclusions.\n- Prospective financial information: based on assumptions about future events and possible actions; evidence on assumptions not unreasonable, proper preparation, presentation and disclosure; no opinion on achievement; \"nothing has come to our attention\"; moderate assurance.\n- Engagement Standards: SAs (audit of HFI), SREs (review of HFI), SAEs (other assurance), SRSs (agreed-upon procedures, compilation). Purpose: high quality standards and guidance.\n- SQC 1: firm's system of quality control; applies to all services covered by Engagement Standards.\n- Standards needed for benchmarks at par with global practice, better reporting, uniformity, knowledge and skill, audit quality.\n- Departure: document how alternatives achieve the purpose, and the reason unless clear; report draws attention; disclosure does not absolve. (Dev's stamp stays undiscounted.)"});
